The Context: Why MLOps Exists
Machine learning models often work well in experimentation but fail when deployed into real‑world systems. This “production gap” happens because:
- Production data behaves differently from training data.
- Manual deployment processes introduce errors.
- Monitoring is missing or insufficient.
- Teams (data science, engineering, DevOps) work in silos.
MLOps addresses these issues by combining DevOps, DataOps, and ML engineering into a unified operational discipline.

2. What MLOps Is and Why It Matters
Definition
MLOps is the set of practices, tools, and processes that manage the end‑to‑end machine learning lifecycle, including:
- Data preparation
- Model training
- Packaging
- Deployment
- Monitoring
- Governance
- Continuous improvement
It aims to make ML systems reliable, scalable, reproducible, and auditable.
Why It’s Strategic
MLOps is not just a technical framework. It:
- Reduces operational risk
- Speeds up innovation
- Ensures compliance
- Improves collaboration
- Enables consistent, repeatable ML delivery
Organisations with strong MLOps capabilities deploy models faster and maintain them more effectively.

3. Case Study: Uber’s Michelangelo Platform
Uber built Michelangelo to solve challenges such as:
- Slow deployment cycles
- Inconsistent model performance
- Lack of standardisation across teams
Michelangelo delivered:
- End‑to‑end MLOps automation
- One‑click deployment
- 5,000+ models in production
- 10 million predictions per second
- Deployment time reduced from months to days
Why This Matters
Michelangelo shows that:
- Centralised platforms reduce fragmentation
- Automation accelerates delivery
- Standardisation improves reliability
- Real‑time ML requires robust infrastructure
These principles can be applied in any organisation, even at smaller scale.

4. Core Components of MLOps
Model Tracking
Captures:
- Experiments
- Hyperparameters
- Metrics
- Dataset versions
- Code versions
This ensures reproducibility and makes debugging easier.
Model Registry
A central repository that stores:
- Model versions
- Metadata
- Approval status
- Deployment history
It enables safe rollbacks and auditability.
CI/CD Pipelines
Automate:
- Data validation
- Model training
- Model testing
- Deployment
This reduces human error and speeds up iteration.
Monitoring & Observability
Tracks:
- Prediction quality
- Data drift
- Concept drift
- Latency and throughput
- System anomalies
Monitoring is essential because ML models degrade silently.
Collaboration & Handover
Ensures smooth transitions between:
- Data scientists
- ML engineers
- DevOps
- Product teams
Clear ownership prevents operational gaps.

5. Automating the ML Lifecycle
Automation supports:
- Scheduled retraining
- Automated validation
- Automated deployment
- Automated rollback
Benefits include:
- Faster updates
- Fewer manual errors
- More consistent performance
- Better reproducibility
Automation is the backbone of modern MLOps.

6. Model Packaging & Deployment
Packaging Options
- Docker: Standardises environments
- ONNX: Optimises models for cross‑platform inference
Deployment Methods
- REST APIs
- Embedded applications
- Cloud ML services (AWS SageMaker, Azure ML, GCP Vertex AI)
Deployment Strategies
- Blue‑Green: Two identical environments; switch traffic when ready
- Canary: Release to a small subset of users first
- A/B Testing: Compare two models simultaneously
These strategies reduce risk and improve user experience.

7. Monitoring in Production
Monitoring ensures models remain accurate and reliable. Key areas include:
Performance Metrics
- Accuracy, precision, recall
- Calibration
- Confidence scores
Data Drift
Input data distribution changes over time.
Concept Drift
The relationship between features and target changes.
System Metrics
- Latency
- Throughput
- Error rates
Alerts should trigger when thresholds are breached.

8. Responding to Model Decay
When performance drops:
- Rollback to a stable version
- Retrain with fresh data
- Tune hyperparameters
- Improve feature engineering
- Investigate data quality issues
Retraining frequency depends on:
- Data volatility
- Business requirements
- Risk tolerance

9. Long‑Term Model Management
Models are not static—they evolve. Long‑term management includes:
Governance
- Documentation
- Model lineage
- Access control
- Compliance (e.g., GDPR, AI Act)
Adaptation
- Continuous monitoring
- Scheduled retraining
- Updating features and pipelines
Collaboration
Cross‑functional alignment ensures models remain useful and safe.

10. Key Takeaways
- MLOps makes ML reliable, scalable, and auditable.
- Automation and monitoring are essential for production success.
- Deployment strategies reduce risk and improve stability.
- Models require ongoing care, not one‑off deployment.
- Cross‑team collaboration is crucial for long‑term impact.

